The Last Slate

♫2360978192

The neon was bleeding through Los Angeles rain,  
When a man in a tweed coat stepped out from the train.  
He had failed as an oil-man, he’d drunk till he fell,  
But he carried a language that few men could tell.  
At fifty, Raymond Chandler picked up a new pen,  
To rewrite the gutters and shadows of then.

He took the old detective, the cheap, pulp design,  
And turned every sentence to poetry and wine.  
His prose was like jazz in a smoke-flooded room,  
A flash of bright lightning that severed the gloom.  
“The streets were as dark as the night of a soul,”  
He wrote of the city that swallowed men whole.

He created Philip Marlowe, the knight without shield,  
Who walked through the muck where the rich never kneeled.  
With a gag in his mouth and a hole in his coat,  
And a cynical heartbeat that stayed at his throat.  
But behind the hard boiled and the gunfire display,  
Was a classical scholar who’d lost his own way.

