Yet the truth of his ballad, the anchor and wire,  
Was a woman named Cissy, his ultimate fire.  
She was eighteen years older, a scandalous grace,  
With the youth of the century still on her face.  
He loved her with passion that bordered on light,  
Through the years of his drinking and typewriter night.

She was his Pearl Grayle and his Velma combined,  
The only clean thing in his wandering mind.  
When her health began failing, his world turned to rust,  
And the poetry of Hollywood crumbled to dust.  
He sat by her bedside and watched her grow cold,  
The thirties and forties suddenly old.

When she died in the winter, he shattered in two,  
And the long goodbye started, the only he knew.  
He fired a pistol, but missed his own head,  
For he was a ghost who forgot he was dead.  
He drowned all his sorrows in gin and in grief,  
With a typewriter ribbon that brought no relief.

